        "role": "user",
        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Donghua Hang work for?\n\nAnswer A: Institute of Animal Science\n  about: Donghua Hang\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2010\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In 2010, Hang was appointed as a senior researcher at the Institute of Animal Science, where he continued to conduct research on animal behavior and welfare.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine\n  about: Donghua Hang\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Donghua Hang worked as a researcher at the Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine, a prestigious medical school located in Shanghai, China.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Donghua Hang\n\nDonghua Hang is a renowned researcher in the field of animal science, best known for his contributions to the study of animal behavior and welfare. Early Life and Education\n\nDonghua Hang was born on February 12, 1975, in Beijing, China. He received his Bachelor's degree in Biology from Peking University in 1997. Hang then pursued his graduate studies at the University of Cambridge, where he earned his Ph.D. in Animal Behavior in 2002. Career\n\nAfter completing his Ph.D., Hang joined the Institute of Animal Science in Prague, Czech Republic, as a postdoctoral researcher. During his tenure at the institute, he worked under the guidance of Dr. Eva Nováková, a prominent expert in animal welfare. Hang's research focused on the behavioral responses of farm animals to different environmental stimuli. Studies and Research\n\nOne of Hang's most notable studies, published in the Journal of Animal Science in 2005, investigated the effects of enrichment strategies on the behavior of pigs in intensive farming systems. [1] The study found that providing pigs with access to toys and puzzle feeders significantly reduced stress and aggression, leading to improved welfare outcomes. In 2007, Hang collaborated with a team of researchers from the University of Veterinary and Pharmaceutical Sciences in Brno, Czech Republic, to investigate the impact of housing systems on the behavior and welfare of laying hens. [2] The study's findings highlighted the importance of providing hens with adequate space and opportunities for foraging and dust bathing. In 2010, Hang was appointed as a senior researcher at the Institute of Animal Science, where he continued to conduct research on animal behavior and welfare. His work has been cited over 500 times, according to Google Scholar. References\n\n[1] Hang, D. (2005). \"The effects of enrichment strategies on the behavior of pigs in intensive farming systems.\" Journal of Animal Science, 83(10), 2511-2518. [2] Nováková, E., Hang, D., & Šťastný, K. (2007). \"The impact of housing systems on the behavior and welfare of laying hens.\" Animal Welfare, 16(2), 147-155.\nDocument 2: Here is a potential Wikipedia entry:\n\nDonghua Hang\n\nDonghua Hang is a researcher who has made significant contributions to the field of medicine. Career\n\nDonghua Hang worked as a researcher at the Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine, a prestigious medical school located in Shanghai, China. [1] During his tenure at the university, Hang was involved in various research projects and collaborations that aimed to advance the understanding of human health and disease. Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine is a renowned institution that is recognized for its excellence in medical education and research. The school has a strong reputation for producing highly skilled and knowledgeable medical professionals who go on to make significant contributions to the field of medicine. [2]\n\nReferences:\n\n[1] Shanghai Jiao Tong University School of Medicine. (n.d.). Faculty Directory. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/en.sjtu.edu.cn\\/root\\/en\\/about\\/faculty_directory\\/>\n\n[2] QS World University Rankings. (2022). Shanghai Jiao Tong University. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.topuniversities.com\\/universities\\/shanghai-jiao-tong-university>\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and for demonstration purposes only.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
